messages:
amanda: Quick question — saw some 401 vs 400 churn in prod. If a request arrives with an Authorization header present but the value is empty (e.g. `Authorization:` or `Authorization: `), what should our server treat that as: missing or invalid?
ryan: Currently middleware checks header presence then parses token. Implementation-wise empty string falls into the parse-failure branch which sometimes maps to 400.
selina: We should standardize. My vote: empty / present-but-empty -> 401 (unauthenticated) and treat unparsable formats like `Bearer null` as 400 (malformed). But we should emit telemetry tag so we can differentiate.
matt: FWIW some proxies (CloudFront, nginx with certain configs) will strip empty headers entirely, so behavior can vary depending on path. If proxy strips it, it's effectively missing and we return 401 today. We need e2e tests for proxy-strip cases.
amanda: Good point about proxies. Also SDKs sometimes send `Authorization: Bearer null` or `Authorization: Bearer ` which is subtly different. We should decide mapping for both.
ryan: Proposal: canonicalize header matching first. If header matches /^Bearer\\s+(\\S+)$/ then token = $1 -> proceed. Else if header present but doesn't match (includes empty token) -> 400 (malformed header). Else (no header) -> 401. Telemetry labels: `auth.missing`, `auth.malformed`, `auth.invalid_token`.
selina: I'm slightly concerned clients expect 401 for empty to trigger refresh/reauth flows. If we send 400 for empty they may not retry. Can we keep 401 for empty but still tag as `auth.malformed`?
ryan: That seems reasonable: distinguish between "present but empty" vs "present but unparsable". Mapping: present-but-empty -> 401 + tag `auth.empty_header`; present-but-unparsable (e.g. `Bearer null` non-whitespace token but fails validation) -> 400 + `auth.malformed_format`; present-and-well-formed-but-invalid-key -> 401 + `auth.invalid_key`.
infra-bot: deploy-checklist: add feature-flag toggle, update auth middleware, add proxy-strip e2e tests, update SDK docs, and add telemetry tags. PR owner: @ryan.
matt: Also callout rotation semantics: during key rotation we accept previous keys for configured grace window; empties should not attempt fallback to org/project headers.
amanda: Action items: 1) agree on status mapping above (ryan's latest), 2) ryan to open PR with tests and feature flag, 3) selina to confirm telemetry naming and add to observability dashboard, 4) matt to add proxy-strip e2e in ingress tests, 5) security to review scope->403 mapping.
ryan: I'll open PR in ~30m. Drafted middleware pseudo:
```js
// auth middleware pseudocode
const authHeader = req.get('authorization');
if (!authHeader) return res.status(401).send('missing');
const m = authHeader.match(/^Bearer\\s+(\\S+)$/);
if (!m) {
  if (authHeader.trim() === '' || /^Bearer\\s*$/.test(authHeader)) {
    telemetry.tag('auth.empty_header');
    return res.status(401).send('empty_authorization');
  }
  telemetry.tag('auth.malformed_format');
  return res.status(400).send('malformed_authorization');
}
const token = m[1];
// validate token -> if invalid: 401 + tag('auth.invalid_key')
```
selina: Looks good. One more: confirm behavior when `X-Project` is present but auth missing. We must not auto-bind request to project unless a valid API key is provided. Add explicit log line so we can debug client confusion.
amanda: Agreed. No silent fallbacks.
ryan: PR opened: https://git.company/infra/auth-empty-header-fix (wip). Adding unit + e2e tests covering CloudFront & nginx header-strip scenarios.
amanda: Perfect. If no objections I'll merge when PR passes CI and security signs off on scope handling. :thumbsup:
